0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","5.9",{"topic":{"id":"5.9","title":"AI-Based Cyber Crime Detection Techniques","minutes":15,"summary":"Evaluate classifiers through the kinds of mistakes they make, the data they learned from, and the decisions they support.","sections":[{"title":"Features, labels, and learning approaches","body":["Supervised learning uses labelled examples, such as messages marked phishing or legitimate. Unsupervised methods look for structure or anomalies without relying on the same labels. Features may include URL characteristics, sender relationships, message structure, or network behavior. Feature choice and label quality can matter more than model complexity.","Data leakage occurs when information unavailable at prediction time influences training or evaluation. Randomly splitting duplicate messages can make results look unrealistically strong. Use time-aware or group-aware validation where appropriate and preserve a genuinely unseen test set. Document how labels were obtained and whether they contain human bias."]},{"title":"Confusion matrix and operational metrics","body":["A true positive is a correctly flagged threat; a false positive is a benign item flagged as threatening; a false negative is a missed threat; a true negative is correctly accepted benign activity. Precision is TP/(TP+FP), recall is TP/(TP+FN), and false-positive rate is FP/(FP+TN). Each denominator answers a different question.","Accuracy can be misleading when threats are rare. A model that labels everything benign may have high accuracy but zero recall. Lowering a threshold can catch more threats while increasing analyst workload and false accusations. Choose a threshold for the operational decision, then evaluate on separate validation data and monitor real performance."]},{"title":"From alert to investigation","body":["A detection score is not the probability that a person committed a crime. It is a model output under particular data and assumptions. Use the alert to prioritize review, preserve the underlying evidence, and test alternative explanations. Explainability can help identify spurious features, but an explanation is not proof of correctness.","Monitor drift, new attack patterns, and feedback loops. If analysts only investigate items the model already flags, missing threats may remain invisible. Periodically sample other traffic and update evaluation responsibly. Keep model versions and decision records so a past alert can be understood later."]}],"practice":{"title":"Choose a threshold for human review","scenario":"The AI lab’s synthetic validation set contains both ordinary and suspicious records.","steps":["Move the decision threshold and observe TP, FP, FN, and TN.","Calculate precision and recall at two settings.","Compare the consequences of missed threats and false alerts.","Recommend a review policy without treating the score as proof."],"debrief":"There is no universally best threshold. The choice depends on harm, prevalence, review capacity, and independent safeguards."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","dpdp"],"sources":["owasp-ml","enisa","bsa"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain features, labels, and learning approaches and apply it to an investigation.","Explain confusion matrix and operational metrics and apply it to an investigation.","Explain from alert to investigation and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.9","prompt":"What does recall measure?","options":["The share of actual positives correctly identified.","The share of all predictions that are correct.","The number of analysts employed.","The probability that the accused is guilty."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Recall is TP divided by all actual positives, TP plus FN.","unit":5}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
